                "word": "animadversiveness",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "5fd2cddf-e830-3abc-baa9-8cfeed210d83",
                        "entry_id": "70710097-6c20-30fc-8694-adca6b9e4934",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The state or quality of being animadversive."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "fd856cc7-f841-4de9-ba57-2fd2054c4d59",
                                "sense_id": "5fd2cddf-e830-3abc-baa9-8cfeed210d83",
                                "text": "[A]nd, better than all—better than the prism-tion of Wellintown—the narrow-mouthishness of Bathos—the animadversiveness of Whistmoreland—and the digastic honesty of Mr. Peile—we have got rid of John Lord Weldon’s ears!!!"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8f2b2569-818a-4ec0-a8ca-2a35f23a398b",
                                "sense_id": "5fd2cddf-e830-3abc-baa9-8cfeed210d83",
                                "text": "I think that the old party who in my childhood used to officiate at our hog-killings as First Royal fat-rendering mendicant must have had a presentiment of what Noah’s book was to contain, from the fact that she remarked to me once that my animadversiveness was remarkable for a child."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e9ed8570-d90e-4dd6-a20b-9c06e4d60a79",
                                "sense_id": "5fd2cddf-e830-3abc-baa9-8cfeed210d83",
                                "text": "Like everybody who tries to do something he is not trained for, they cause a good deal of trouble by their interference, sometimes to themselves, and sometimes, as in the instance under consideration, to innocent people, who are wounded by this animadversiveness on their conduct."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "c55b231a-c2f6-4eda-88db-c41ca40be3c6",
                                "sense_id": "5fd2cddf-e830-3abc-baa9-8cfeed210d83",
                                "text": "f it were possible to be a true artist or only a critic without ideality and heart—if great animadversiveness and knowledge of art, refined taste and improved technical ability, were sufficient—then the Emperor Nero had been what he prided himself on being."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
